# Debugging Laravel 8 Pagination UI Mismatches

When upgrading to Laravel 8, developers often encounter a specific frustration with the Blade pagination component: the rendered links look unstyled or "messy." This issue typically stems from a disconnect between the CSS framework you are using and the default HTML structure generated by `->links()`. The source code provided shows a classic setup where Bootstrap is expected to handle the styling, but the pagination output fails to apply those styles correctly.

The root cause usually lies in how the `links()` method renders its HTML versus what your view expects. In Laravel 8, the default pagination view (located at `resources/views/vendor/pagination/default.blade.php`) generates a list of anchor tags and spans with specific classes like `disabled` and `active`. However, if you are relying on Bootstrap to style these elements, the generated markup might be missing the necessary utility classes or structure that Bootstrap requires to render flexbox layouts correctly.

Looking at the provided Blade template, there is a subtle syntax error in the container for the pagination links:

```blade
<div class="d-felx justify-content-center">
    {{ $posts->links() }}
</div>
```

The CSS class `d-felx` contains a typo; it should be `d-flex`. While this specific typo would prevent Bootstrap from applying flexbox properties, rendering the pagination items in a horizontal row with proper spacing, fixing the spelling alone does not guarantee the component looks right. The default Laravel pagination view often includes its own internal styling for the "previous" and "next" buttons using Tailwind-like classes or inline styles that conflict with Bootstrap's utility-first approach if not configured correctly.

To resolve this in a way that integrates cleanly with your existing `PostsController` logic, you should switch from the default paginator to the Bootstrap-specific view provided by Laravel. This ensures the generated HTML matches the class names Bootstrap expects. You can do this by modifying the controller's return statement or by explicitly specifying the view path.

Update your `PostsController` index method to use the Bootstrap pagination view:

```php
use App\Models\Posts;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\View;

class PostsController extends Controller
{
    public function index()
    {
        $posts = Posts::latest()->paginate(6);

        // Force the use of the Bootstrap pagination view
        return view('post.index', compact('posts'))->withPagination('bootstrap');
    }
}
```

Alternatively, you can override the default view directly in your `resources/views/vendor/pagination/bootstrap-4.blade.php` (or bootstrap-5 depending on your version) by copying the standard Bootstrap pagination markup there. This approach gives you full control over the HTML structure without needing to modify the controller logic every time.

Another common pitfall involves the placement of the pagination component. In the provided code, the pagination is placed inside a `div` with `justify-content-center`. If you are using Bootstrap 4 or 5, ensure that your main container has the correct breakpoint classes applied (e.g., `container-fluid` vs `container`) and that the flex utilities are applied to a parent element that actually supports them. The default Laravel pagination view creates an unordered list (`<ul>`) which is a block-level element. If this list is not wrapped correctly, it might break out of its intended container or fail to center properly.

If you prefer to stick with the default Laravel view, you may need to manually add Bootstrap classes to the layout or create a custom pagination view that wraps the output in a `<nav>` tag and applies `justify-content-center` to the list itself. For example:

```blade
<div class="d-flex justify-content-center my-4">
    {{ $posts->appends(['page' => request()->get('page')])->links('pagination::bootstrap-4') }}
</div>
```

Note that in newer versions of Laravel, the `links()` method accepts a second argument to specify the view name. If you are on a version where this is not supported out of the box, ensure you have the correct vendor views registered or created.

Finally, always verify that your Blade syntax for the loop and the pagination output is consistent. The code snippet uses `$post->title` and `$post->body`, which assumes the collection contains models with those attributes. If the `Posts` model casts dates to strings instead of Carbon instances, the `diffForHumans()` helper will fail. Ensure your model definition includes `$casts = ['created_at' => 'datetime'];` or that you are accessing raw database strings appropriately.

By aligning the pagination view with your CSS framework and correcting markup inconsistencies like the typo in the flex class, the pagination UI will render cleanly without custom hacking. For more complex layout requirements or if you need to maintain a specific design system, consider utilizing the `withQueryString` helper to preserve query parameters when navigating between pages.
